Scene: DigitalHuman.olo
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIGITAL HUMAN fixture (issue #1222, the epic's own acceptance).
#
# The first scene in this project on which the SKIN, EYE and ORAL children run
# at the same time on one subject: #1241 on the cranium and the ears, #1244 on
# the globes and tear lines, #1245 on the lips, gums, teeth and tongue.
#
# WHAT THE CRANIUM DOES NOT DO, AND WHY IT IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN DISCOVERED.
# ReferenceHead.oloskin — the profile the cranium and both ears carry, and the
# one ReferenceHead.olo and Eyes.olo also name — is authored at TRANSPORT
# VERSION 1 (ScreenSpaceDiffusion). The ladder is cumulative, so version 1 is
# diffusion and NOTHING ABOVE IT: no #1242 thin-region transmission, no #1243
# layered specular. #1239 wrote that feature owners would upgrade the fixtures
# they landed against, and for those two issues that upgrade never happened.
#
# The practical consequence, measured in this scene: under the backlight rig
# the material-transmission debug view is essentially black over the cranium
# and both ears, because the term is not enabled on their profile rather than
# because it is broken. The ears are still built as a thin region (1.5 mm
# against the cheek's 6 mm) so that they become the subject the moment the
# profile moves past version 1 — which is a one-field edit to that .oloskin.
#
# The oral and ocular parts DO exercise the upper ladder: OralLip, OralGum,
# OralTongue and OralEnamel are version 4 and EyeIris and EyeTearLine are
# version 5, all with a transmission strength authored. Reported on #1222.
#
# THIS SCENE SPENDS THE ENTIRE SKIN-PROFILE SLOT BUDGET, and that is a finding
# rather than a detail. Seven distinct .oloskin profiles are named here
# (ReferenceHead, EyeIris, EyeTearLine, OralLip, OralTongue, OralGum,
# OralEnamel) and the G-Buffer's slot field is THREE BITS with the all-ones
# pattern reserved for "no profile" — so kMaxSkinProfileSlots is 7
# (Renderer/SkinProfile.h) and a complete face fills it exactly. An EIGHTH
# profile anywhere in a scene containing this head does not fail loudly: it
# resolves to kSkinProfileSlotNone with SlotBudgetFull, logs once, and renders
# as not-skin. A second character, a different eye colour, or a freckled
# variant of this head would each be that eighth. Reported on #1222 rather than
# worked around here.
#
# WHY A NEW SCENE AND NOT AN EDIT TO ReferenceHead.olo. That fixture is a PINNED
# BENCHMARK whose captures are golden and whose manifest is asserted
# (ReferenceFixtureManifestTest); adding geometry to it would invalidate every
# number measured against it. #1244's Eyes.olo and #1245's OralSurfaces.olo each
# made that same call and each stayed additive. This scene is the third and last
# in that family, and it is the one that puts the other two together — which is
# precisely the epic criterion neither of them could satisfy alone.
#
# WHY PRIMITIVES, STILL. Every claim these features support is a claim about the
# MATERIAL, and a material does not know what mesh it is on. What the profiles
# need from geometry is a uniformly scaled sphere for each globe, a thin region
# for the transmission to cross, and a cavity for the oral occlusion to darken —
# all of which primitives give without a licensed asset. A scanned head dropped
# in here changes the subject and nothing else. That head remains a genuine
# external dependency; see docs/guides/benchmark-reference-fixtures.md.
#
# WHAT THIS SCENE IS FOR, AND WHAT IT IS NOT FOR.
#   FOR: the LIVE half of the epic's verification grid. The material debug views
#        (diffuse / specular / transmission / profile identity) are deferred-only
#        and are reachable here through the MCP renderer tools, which is how the
#        Vulkan rows of that grid get captured at all — the headless fixtures
#        need a real GL 4.6 context and skip without one.
#   NOT FOR: the tone ladder or the expression. Several skin tones need several
#        authored profiles and an expression needs a morph target, and both are
#        measured headlessly and assertively by
#        OloEngine/tests/Rendering/PropertyTests/SkinDigitalHumanEvidenceTest.cpp.
#        Duplicating them here as a static pose would look like coverage and
#        assert nothing.
#
# THE THREE RIGS ARE ALL PRESENT AND ONLY ONE IS STRONG. Criterion 2 asks for
# soft, hard side and backlight, and a scene can only be authored in one of them
# — so all three lights exist and the other two are dimmed rather than deleted.
# Moving between rigs is then an INTENSITY edit on lights that are already
# placed, which keeps the geometry, the exposure and the shadow settings fixed
# across a rig change. Changing the rig by adding a light would change two
# things and make the comparison unreadable.
#
# THE EARS ARE BUILT FOR THE BACKLIGHT, and are waiting on a profile. Thin
# ellipsoids with a real ThicknessFactor, standing proud of the cranium:
# #1242's transmission is a thin-region term and a head with no thin region
# backlit is just a dark head. They do not glow today for the version-1 reason
# above, not for a geometric one.
#
# EVERY PART IS PROUD OF THE CRANIUM ON PURPOSE. The head is a solid ellipsoid
# with no mouth aperture, so anything authored *inside* it is simply invisible
# — the first version of this scene buried both globes, both gum strips, both
# tooth blocks and the tongue, and rendered a bald sphere with lips. Each
# part's z is now set so its front face clears the cranium surface at that
# (x, y), in the order lips > teeth > gums > tongue > throat.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PostProcessSettings:
  TonemapOperator: 2
  Exposure: 1
  Gamma: 2.2
  BloomEnabled: true
  # Low threshold, modest intensity — same reasoning as Eyes.olo: the corneal
  # catchlight is the brightest thing here by a wide margin, and a little bloom
  # is what makes it read as wet rather than as a white dot.
  BloomThreshold: 1.1
  BloomIntensity: 0.18
  BloomIterations: 5
  VignetteEnabled: false
  # FXAA OFF, like every measurement fixture in this project: a pixel A/B
  # against an anti-aliased frame measures the filter as much as the feature.
  FXAAEnabled: false
  SSAOEnabled: true
  SSAORadius: 0.2
  SSAOBias: 0.025
  SSAOIntensity: 1
  SSAOSamples: 32
  # SSR OFF. A wet coat and a cornea are the two most reflective things in the
  # scene, and a screen-space reflection on top of them would make "is that the
  # coat or the SSR?" unanswerable from a capture.
  SSREnabled: false
  SSGIEnabled: false
  AutoExposureEnabled: false
FogSettings:
  Enabled: false
WindSettings:
  Enabled: false
Entities:
  # -- Camera: a three-quarter head-and-shoulders, not a dead-on portrait. A
  #    head-on view is the one pose where the corneal refraction and a painted
  #    iris coincide by symmetry, and where a grazing-angle specular is
  #    smallest. Both features are judged off-axis. --
  - Entity: 1222000001000001
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Camera
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0.17, 0.03, 0.30]
      Rotation: [-0.05, 0.52, 0]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    CameraComponent:
      Camera:
        ProjectionType: 0
        PerspectiveFOV: 0.7854
        PerspectiveNear: 0.01
        PerspectiveFar: 100
        OrthographicSize: 10
        OrthographicNear: -1
        OrthographicFar: 1
      Primary: true
      FixedAspectRatio: false
  # -- SOFT KEY (the authored rig). Broad, front-above, shadows ON so the
  #    terminator across the cheek is real. This is the rig the scene opens in.
  #    To switch rigs: raise one of the two below and drop this one. --
  - Entity: 1222000001000002
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Soft Key
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0.22, 0.30, 0.42]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    PointLightComponent:
      Color: [1, 0.97, 0.93]
      Intensity: 6
      Range: 3
      CastShadows: true
  # -- HARD SIDE (dimmed). Almost in the head's own plane, which is where the
  #    layered specular of #1243 and the pore band are legible. Raise to ~9 and
  #    drop the soft key to ~0.5 for the hard-side cell. --
  - Entity: 1222000001000003
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Hard Side
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [-0.46, 0.04, 0.06]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    PointLightComponent:
      Color: [1, 0.96, 0.90]
      Intensity: 0.6
      Range: 3
      CastShadows: true
  # -- BACKLIGHT (dimmed). Behind and slightly above, pointing back at the lens.
  #    Raise to ~11 and drop the other two to ~0.3 for the backlight cell: this
  #    is the only rig under which #1242's transmission through the ears is what
  #    the picture is made of. --
  - Entity: 1222000001000004
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Backlight
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0.05, 0.16, -0.40]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    PointLightComponent:
      Color: [0.94, 0.95, 1]
      Intensity: 0.8
      Range: 3
      CastShadows: false
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THE CRANIUM — #1241 diffusion, #1242 transmission, #1243 layered specular,
  # all from ReferenceHead.oloskin. The same profile handle ReferenceHead.olo
  # and Eyes.olo name, deliberately: the epic's claim is that these features
  # compose, and re-authoring the skin for this scene would have tested a
  # different material than the one the other two fixtures measure.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - Entity: 1222000001000010
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Cranium
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0, 0, 0]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [0.098, 0.115, 0.105]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.66, 0.49, 0.42]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.42
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 15952688685437936278
      # 6 mm: a cheek. Not the ears' thickness — a uniform head-wide value would
      # make the ears stop being the thin region and the backlight cell stop
      # showing anything the rest of the head does not.
      ThicknessFactor: 0.006
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THE EARS. Thin slabs, standing proud, at 1.5 mm — a quarter of the cheek's
  # thickness, which is roughly the real ratio. These are what the backlight
  # rig is aimed at.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - Entity: 1222000001000011
    TagComponent:
      Tag: LeftEar
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [-0.096, 0.012, -0.008]
      Rotation: [0, 0.22, 0.12]
      Scale: [0.012, 0.038, 0.026]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.70, 0.50, 0.44]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.40
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 15952688685437936278
      ThicknessFactor: 0.0015
  - Entity: 1222000001000012
    TagComponent:
      Tag: RightEar
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0.096, 0.012, -0.008]
      Rotation: [0, -0.22, -0.12]
      Scale: [0.012, 0.038, 0.026]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.70, 0.50, 0.44]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.40
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 15952688685437936278
      ThicknessFactor: 0.0015
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THE GLOBES — #1244, EyeIris.oloskin. Same profile, UNIFORMLY SCALED, and
  # converged on a point just in front of the lens: unequal non-zero yaws so one
  # capture shows the refraction at two strengths. All three of those are that
  # fixture's stated requirements, honoured here rather than re-derived.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - Entity: 1222000001000020
    TagComponent:
      Tag: LeftEye
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [-0.031, 0.021, 0.092]
      Rotation: [0, 0.10, 0]
      Scale: [0.0123, 0.0123, 0.0123]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.78, 0.74, 0.71]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.09
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 1244000000000000001
      ThicknessFactor: 0.004
  - Entity: 1222000001000021
    TagComponent:
      Tag: RightEye
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0.031, 0.021, 0.092]
      Rotation: [0, -0.16, 0]
      Scale: [0.0123, 0.0123, 0.0123]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.78, 0.74, 0.71]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.09
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 1244000000000000001
      ThicknessFactor: 0.004
  # -- The tear lines: EyeTearLine.oloskin, version 5 with the eye OFF. Flattened
  #    in Y, which is what makes the highlight a LINE and not a second
  #    catchlight. --
  - Entity: 1222000001000022
    TagComponent:
      Tag: LeftTearLine
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [-0.031, 0.011, 0.099]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [0.011, 0.0013, 0.004]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 1
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.74, 0.58, 0.53]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.06
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 1244000000000000002
      ThicknessFactor: 0.0004
  - Entity: 1222000001000023
    TagComponent:
      Tag: RightTearLine
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0.031, 0.011, 0.099]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [0.011, 0.0013, 0.004]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 1
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.74, 0.58, 0.53]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.06
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 1244000000000000002
      ThicknessFactor: 0.0004
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THE ORAL REGION — #1245. Four profiles, and the reason there are four rather
  # than one is that issue's own criterion: teeth and mucosa must not be
  # assigned identical skin response. Enamel differs from gum by its coat index
  # (1.63 against saliva's 1.33), which is one authored field and no second code
  # path.
  #
  # THE MOUTH IS AUTHORED SLIGHTLY PARTED. A closed mouth hides the gums, the
  # teeth and the tongue behind the lips, so the default screenshot of this
  # scene would show one of the four oral profiles and the other three would be
  # present, shading, and invisible.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - Entity: 1222000001000030
    TagComponent:
      Tag: UpperLip
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0, -0.038, 0.095]
      Rotation: [0.12, 0, 0]
      Scale: [0.030, 0.0072, 0.010]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.62, 0.32, 0.31]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.26
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 1245000000000000001
      ThicknessFactor: 0.004
  - Entity: 1222000001000031
    TagComponent:
      Tag: LowerLip
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0, -0.057, 0.095]
      Rotation: [-0.10, 0, 0]
      Scale: [0.030, 0.0085, 0.011]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.64, 0.33, 0.32]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.26
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 1245000000000000001
      ThicknessFactor: 0.004
  # -- The throat: the CAVITY the occlusion term exists to darken. Set back
  #    behind the teeth, so a coat that brightened the diffuse half would show
  #    up here as an interior that glows — which is the failure #1245's cavity
  #    weight was added to prevent, and the one thing this scene can show at a
  #    glance. --
  - Entity: 1222000001000032
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Throat
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0, -0.047, 0.086]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [0.020, 0.008, 0.012]
    MeshComponent:
      Primitive: 2
    MaterialComponent:
      AlbedoColor: [0.40, 0.17, 0.16]
      Metallic: 0
      Roughness: 0.34
      MaterialKind: 2
      SkinProfile: 1245000000000000002
      ThicknessFactor: 0.006
  - Entity: 1222000001000033
